Eitri @VERSION@ -- Your Neovim, tiled with your agents, terminals and the web pages you build, in one window.
Built from commit @COMMIT@ in the Ubuntu 24.04 release container. It runs on glibc 2.39, GTK 4.14
and WebKitGTK 2.40 (the webkitgtk-6.0 API) or newer.

0.3.0 is the tiling release. The window becomes a keyboard-driven tiling manager, Codex and any agent that speaks
ACP join Claude Code, and you can put web pages of your own in the window. A Claude Code tab now also shows what
Claude Code does on its own: background tasks, workflows, the turns it starts by itself and the questions it asks
you. Your Neovim stays the editor: a real `nvim --embed` with your own `init.lua`, plugins, LSP and keymaps.

## What's new in 0.3.0

**Your Neovim**

- Unchanged at the core: Eitri draws your own Neovim with a fork of Neovide's renderer and never replaces it. The
  editor is one module of the window, and every other module sits beside it.
- The window title names the project (`myproject — Eitri · docs`).

**Opening a file**

- `eitri FILE` opens a single file. The project comes from the file: its git repository, or its folder when there is
  none, or your current directory when that directory holds the file and lies inside the repository. Several
  files, `+N` and `+` before the first file, `FILE:LINE` and `FILE:LINE:COL`, `--` and `eitri DIR FILE` work as
  `nvim` users expect; `+page.svelte` and similar are refused as nvim commands.
- When a window of that project is already open, the file opens in it, the window comes to the front and the
  command returns at once. The window has to be on the project worked out for the file: from a subfolder of a
  repository that is the subfolder. Typed in Eitri's own bottom terminal, it opens in that window. `--new-window`
  always starts a new window; so do `eitri DIR`, `--clean` and `--account`.
- A file with no project of its own (`eitri ~/notes.md`, `eitri /etc/hosts`, `eitri /tmp/x.py`) opens in its folder
  with no project boundary: a Claude tab asks before every file and command, a Codex tab can still write anywhere
  in that folder, and the panel says so in a note.
- On GNOME a command typed in a terminal cannot raise the window (GNOME wants an activation token it does not
  have), so GNOME may only show a "ready" notice. Only sway was tried; the raise on GNOME has not been.
- Eitri now appears under Open With for plain text, Markdown, C, C++, Python, Rust, JSON, TOML and shell scripts
  (it does not become the default); that listing has not been tried in a file manager. `eitri split FILE` and
  `eitri panel FILE` are not supported: both still take a directory.

**Keyboard-driven tiling**

- Splits, tabbed and stacked containers (`<prefix> Alt+w` / `Alt+s`), floats (`<prefix> Alt+Shift+Space`), a
  scratchpad (`<prefix> Alt+_` puts a module away, `Alt+-` brings it back) and named workspaces (`<prefix> (`, `)`,
  `L`, `s`, `$`; `main` always stays). i3's moves are there as Alt chords after the prefix.
- tmux's prefix keys, with `Ctrl+b` as the prefix and 36 new keys after it: layouts (`Alt+3` to `Alt+5`, `Space`,
  `E`), rotate (`Ctrl+o`, `Alt+o`), mark and swap (`m`, `M`), move to an edge (`H`, `J`, `K`), pane numbers (`q`),
  moving a module (`Alt+H/J/K/L`), selecting a container (`Alt+a`, `Alt+d`), breaking a chat tab out into a view of
  its own (`!`), and each pane's own text size (`Ctrl+=` / `Ctrl+-` / `Ctrl+0`). With `keymap.from_tmux` on, Eitri
  reads more of your `~/.tmux.conf` than before: the layout commands, `rotate-window`, `display-panes`,
  `break-pane`, `switch-client -n/-p/-l`, `choose-tree -s`, `select-pane -m/-M`, `swap-pane` and
  `split-window -c "#{pane_current_path}"`.
- Up to nine terminals (`<prefix> Alt+Enter`), each with its own shell, and up to nine chat views side by side.
- In Lua: `eitri.layout.default`, `.workspace`, `.rule` (where a new module opens), `.open`, `.float`, `.run`, and
  `eitri.layout.export()`, which returns the window you arranged as the `init.lua` calls that make it.
- The layout file is now version 2. The old file is read once and left as it was, so going back to 0.2.2 is safe.
- For i3 or sway with `$mod = Alt`, `docs/keymap/i3-alt-mod.lua` moves the rows your window manager would take.
- `:EitriTutor` in the editor teaches the window's keys hands-on, in English or Simplified Chinese, on Neovim's own
  tutor. The Keys page of the guide now opens with the prefix basics.

**Pages you build** (experimental)

- A Markdown preview ships with Eitri, off until you add `eitri.plugin.load("markdown")` to `init.lua`. It
  renders as you type (GitHub-flavoured Markdown, KaTeX, Mermaid, alerts) and scrolls in step with Neovim both
  ways. `https` images in a document load unless you set `remote_images = "off"`.
- Your own pages: a Lua panel (`eitri.panel.register`) shows an HTML file from `~/.config/eitri/panels/`, or any
  address such as your development server, as a module of the window. A plugin is a directory with an
  `eitri-plugin.toml`; its page gets only the host powers the manifest lists, Eitri's colours and the same keys as
  the Markdown preview. The guide's new "Pages you build" page shows both, with a complete small plugin.
- **Experimental:** the manifest and the page bridge may change during 0.x, and there is no consent screen yet:
  loading a plugin trusts it as you trust any program you run.

**Agents you supervise**

- Claude Code tabs request Claude Code's own system prompt (the one `claude -p` uses), plus a short
  note about Eitri's panel; **Codex tabs** (`:tabnew codex`, with `codex` 0.160.0 or newer, always in Codex's
  `workspace-write` sandbox); and **any ACP agent** you register with `eitri.agent.register{...}`. Each kind asks its
  own trust question about the project files it would load.
- **Manual mode**: in a Claude Code tab every file edit is a card you answer, and your saved rules still apply.
  In a Codex tab, Manual makes each request to leave Codex's sandbox a card, while edits and commands inside the
  sandbox still run without asking (the agents guide says more); ACP tabs have no Manual. `Shift+Tab` cycles
  Manual, Auto and Bypass; `agent.default_mode` sets where a new tab starts.
- **Enter while a turn runs steers it** in Claude Code and Codex tabs: the agent reads your message at its next
  step. An ACP tab holds the message until the turn ends. `Ctrl+Enter` interrupts the turn and sends at once.
- The chat page is redone: one quiet column, frames only around what you review or answer, the agent's thinking
  shown as you choose (`agent.thinking`), `Worked for 8s` under each finished turn, and a `/` menu with the
  agent's own commands.
- Turn review counts Codex and ACP edits, reverts a single hunk by checking only that hunk's lines, and `y` on a
  file row copies its path.
- The trust question shows everything whole, and also covers `CLAUDE.md`, `.mcp.json` and rules in the
  directories above the repository.

**Claude Code's own work, in the tab**

- A turn Claude Code starts without you is shown like yours. When a background command finishes, a monitor
  reports, a subagent or workflow ends, or a scheduled wake-up fires, the tab shows what started the turn
  (`Background command "build" completed`, `Scheduled wake-up: <reason>`), then its tool calls, cards and reply.
  A message you send while it runs goes into that turn, and one Claude Code keeps for later waits as a `↪` line
  until it is read.
- Background work has a list. The band counts it in Claude Code's words (`2 shells, 1 monitor`), and `/tasks`
  lists every background shell, monitor, subagent and workflow: `Enter` shows a task's detail and the end of its
  output, `x` stops that one task, and `o` opens its output read-only in Neovim.
- When a dynamic workflow needs your approval, its card shows the name, arguments and script (`View raw script`
  opens it in Neovim). While one runs, the band shows how many of its agents are done, and `/workflows` lists its
  phases and agents.
- A foreground subagent's calls are nested under its row; a background subagent takes one status line, and its
  report starts a turn of its own.
- Claude Code's notices appear where its terminal shows them: `Compacting conversation...` and then a divider,
  the retry while the model service is overloaded, and the words of a `UserPromptSubmit` hook that blocked a prompt.
- `/clear` starts a new conversation in the same tab. Background tasks keep running, and a resume continues the
  new conversation.
- When Claude asks you a multiple-choice question (`AskUserQuestion`), a question box takes the place of the message
  box. You pick with the number keys or `j`/`k`, can type an answer of your own (`Ctrl+g` edits it in Neovim), add
  notes to an option with a preview, and send with `Ctrl+Enter`. Only you answer: bypass, `Ctrl+y`, the card keys
  and saved rules never touch a question.
- A resumed conversation follows Claude Code's own turns: turns it started by itself come back with their cause,
  the `/` and `!` commands you typed come back as your prompts, and questions come back with your answers.
- Eitri raises Claude Code's longest allowed command timeout (`BASH_MAX_TIMEOUT_MS`) to 2147483647 ms, so a
  background command Claude starts with a long timeout keeps running. A background command started without a
  timeout is still stopped after 30 minutes, Claude Code's own default, which Eitri keeps on purpose; a value you
  set yourself wins.
- Not in this release: plan mode, attaching files or images to a message, the terminal's `Ctrl+B` that moves a
  running command to the background (in Eitri `Ctrl+b` is the prefix), and a list of scheduled wake-ups and cron
  tasks. Those still fire, each as a turn of its own, while the tab stays open; closing the tab ends them, except a
  cron task Claude Code was told to keep across sessions, which it saves in the project and may run in a later
  session there.

The agents guide's first section describes all of this.

**Lighter at rest**

- An idle window draws nothing, and the panel wakes only when it has something to draw. The bottom terminal echoes
  a key without waiting for a frame, and a long scrollback costs far less to draw.

**Companion mode on Hyprland**

- Focus moves and raises now work on Hyprland's Lua config (0.55 and later) and on 0.57, which removed the legacy
  `hyprctl dispatch` names Eitri sent: `Ctrl+h/j/k/l` at the panel's edge, nvim's edge key toward the panel,
  `:EitriPanel` and the hand-back to the editor did nothing there. Eitri now asks Hyprland once which config it
  runs and writes its commands to match, and a command Hyprland refuses is written to the log once. Run on a live
  Hyprland 0.57.0, and 0.56.2 with `hyprland.conf` and with `hyprland.lua`. The second window's raises have not
  been run on a live Hyprland.

**What this can break:**

- **Your `init.lua` may stop Eitri at startup** if it `set`s one of the new prefix keys, as every `set` over a
  default does; the message names the key. The new keys, in the spelling `eitri.keymap` takes: `m`, `M`, `q`, `E`,
  `H`, `J`, `K`, `L`, `s`, `Space`, `(`, `)`, `$`, `!`, `C-o`, `C-=`, `C-+`, `C--`, `C-0`, `M-3`, `M-4`, `M-5`,
  `M-e`, `M-o`, `M-w`, `M-s`, `M-a`, `M-d`, `M-H`, `M-J`, `M-K`, `M-L`, `M-Enter`, `M-S-Space`, `M--` and `M-_`. Put
  `k.del("prefix", "<key>")` before that `set`. With the tmux import on and your tmux configuration binding the
  key, no `del` is needed. A `del` of a key that is not bound also stops startup, so add these lines when you
  upgrade, not before. A Lua panel or plugin keyed `H`, `J` or `K` now stops startup too.
- **`init.lua` is read from `$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/eitri`** when that variable is set to an absolute path, and from
  `~/.config/eitri` otherwise (`EITRI_CONFIG_DIR` still comes first); 0.2.2 read `~/.config/eitri` whatever
  `XDG_CONFIG_HOME` said. If your `XDG_CONFIG_HOME` points elsewhere, move your configuration there.
  `EITRI_CONFIG_DIR` must now be an absolute path: a relative one stops startup, and an empty one counts as unset.
- **`Shift+Tab` cycles three modes**: Manual, Auto, Bypass, then Manual again. Bypass is entered only from Auto,
  so leaving it lands in Manual (in Auto in an ACP tab, which has no Manual).
- **Every project you trusted asks once more**, because the question now shows more than it did.
- **In the message box, `Ctrl+a` and `Ctrl+e` move to the start and end of the line**, as in a shell; they no
  longer select everything.
- The panel's typing mode is now called INSERT (it was INPUT).
- **In a Claude Code tab, `Ctrl+c` no longer stops background work.** It interrupts the turn; background commands,
  subagents and workflows keep running. Stop one from `/tasks` with `x`.
- **Closing a Claude Code tab, a chat view or the window asks first while background tasks run** or a question
  waits, and says what would stop. Scheduled wake-ups and cron tasks are not counted: closing ends them without
  asking, apart from a cron task Claude Code was told to keep across sessions.
- **In a Manual tab, Claude Code's scheduling and task tools no longer ask** (`ScheduleWakeup`, the cron tools,
  `ListAgents`, `TaskStop`); `SendMessage` still does.
- **A folder that is no boundary now makes a Claude tab ask, however it was opened.** That is your home directory
  or above it, a folder you do not own, a folder other users can write to, the filesystem root and the kernel
  trees. `eitri DIR` on a tree others can write to, on one you do not own (another user's checkout, a container
  running as root over a mounted checkout, some network mounts), or on a 0775 checkout whose group Eitri can find
  only through NSS (not in `/etc/group`), used to run as a project and now asks before every file and command.
  A checkout that is `drwxrwxr-x you you` with a group that only you belong to is unchanged. The panel shows a note
  that says why.
- **An Auto tab at a home directory now asks.** Where Claude Code runs its own auto mode, an Auto tab used to leave
  every call to Claude Code's classifier even at `eitri ~`; at a folder that is no boundary Eitri's own rules answer
  instead, as in a Manual tab, and put each call that is judged against the project on a card. Bypass is unchanged,
  and so are Codex tabs: they keep their `workspace-write` sandbox over that whole folder.
- **`eitri FILE` when a window of the project is open hands the file to that window** instead of starting one;
  `--new-window` is the way to get a new window.
- **A mistyped path can open a window.** `eitri typo` used to stop with `no such directory`. In a folder that exists
  it now opens an empty buffer named `typo`, with the project found from that folder, and `:w` creates the file. A
  path in a folder that does not exist still stops the launch.
- **A second Eitri window on the same project keeps its own layout and saved tabs.** It starts from the first
  window's layout and then saves only its own, so closing it no longer changes what the first window opens with.

## Upgrading from 0.2.2

- **The agent backend is built again, once.** 0.3.0 pins a newer Verdandi (@VERDANDI_REV@), so `install.sh`
  builds the new backend before it switches over, and after a `.deb`/`.rpm` upgrade you run `eitri setup` before
  your first turn. It downloads Node.js and the Claude Agent SDK from npm again (about 600 MiB of disk).
- A Claude Code conversation started in Eitri before 0.3.0 **keeps** its earlier, shorter instructions when you
  continue it (measured with Claude Code 2.1.302); a new conversation gets the full ones, and `/compact` may
  refresh them too (not yet measured).
- Your window opens as you left it. Its 0.2.2 layout file is read once into the new one and left as it was, so
  0.2.2 still opens with it.

**`v0.2.0` will stop building from git.** The public history of the Neovide fork was rewritten after 0.2.0
(only commit metadata changed; the files are the same), so the `v0.2.0` tag and every earlier public commit
point at fork commits that no branch holds any more. Build 0.2.1 or later from git. For 0.2.0 itself, its
own `install.sh` (the 0.2.0 release asset) with `--from-source` works only until GitHub stops serving those
old objects, and its `eitri-0.2.0-source.tar.gz` release asset, which carries the fork's files, rebuilds it
for good; later releases' `install.sh` cannot build 0.2.0 from source. The 0.2.0 packages, tarball and
`install.sh` are not affected.

## How much of this has been tried

Everything new has automated tests. The tiling window (containers, floats, the scratchpad, workspaces, chat
views and open rules) and the Markdown preview passed every row of their checks in an isolated, headless test
session: scripted keys, not a person at a real desktop. The Claude Code backend's suites that run the real `claude`
against the model service (2.1.302; 113 cases) last ran on a development build with an earlier Verdandi than the
one this release pins. They ran in two sessions because the first stopped when the test account
reached the model service's rate limit; after the second, all 113 pass. Two cases failed along the way for reasons other than this
release's change: one hit the model service's usage-policy check on a probe that asks for a destructive command (it
passed when run again), and one met this CLI version's new transcript line types, which the history reader now skips.
Eight Glob and Grep probes in one suite are skipped, because this CLI build lists no such tools. The request a Claude Code tab
sends was captured from that same earlier backend against a local stand-in: it carries `claude -p`'s system prompt
with Claude Code's own identity line for the Agent SDK and Eitri's note once at its end. No turn against the real
model service has run on this release's backend; what it adds was checked against a scripted stand-in, as the next
paragraph says. Codex tabs passed their scripted suites with `codex` 0.160.0 to 0.161.0 (a
newer Codex runs and is marked untested), and the real `codex` 0.162 and 0.163 were run against a scripted model.
`eitri FILE` passed 34 checks in the same kind of headless sway session, with the real launcher and a scripted Claude Code
stand-in: a file in a repository, `+42`, `~/notes.md` with its note and a card in a Manual and in an Auto tab, a file
handed to a running window from outside and from its bottom terminal, a window that cannot take it, and a saved layout that
hides the editor. A person has not looked at all its screens, and GNOME's raise, a Codex tab at such a folder, a scaled
output and real hardware have not been tried.
Companion mode on Hyprland (0.57.0 and 0.56.2) was run on a live desktop in a virtual machine, with keys sent through QEMU; on niri, KDE or a real GNOME login it has not been seen on a screen yet.

The Claude Code tab's handling of Claude Code's own work was tested on this release's backend with the real Claude
Code 2.1.311 against a scripted stand-in for the model service. The flows above ran end to end through Eitri's agent
host, and fifteen screen checks of them passed in the redesigned chat page in the isolated, headless session, in dark
and light and at a narrow panel width: turns Claude Code started, `/tasks` and `/workflows`, the workflow card,
subagents, compaction, a message steered into a running turn, `/clear`, a scheduled wake-up, retries and the
question box, where an answer typed through an input method was sent. Restoring a resumed conversation was also
compared with real Claude Code session files. Steering was seen on a screen only in a Claude Code tab, and typing
through an input method into the companion panel, in its message box or its question box, has not been verified.

None of 0.3.0 has been through real hardware and a person's hands yet, so it is not yet stable enough for everyday
use. That waits for real hardware and a week of daily use, not for a version number; reports from real desktops
are especially welcome. What you may run into is listed in the known issues (`docs/known-issues.md` in the
repository).

## Performance, against native Neovide

Measured on development builds shortly before this release, in an isolated, headless test session on an AMD
desktop, and on an Intel laptop where it says so:

- **On GTK 4.24, typing is one refresh slower than native Neovide.** In a headless session with the compositor
  feeding GTK a real 165 Hz period through presentation feedback, GTK 4.24.1 added 5.97 ms at the median;
  the same build with GTK 4.22.5 added 0.30 ms. With GTK's 60 Hz fallback clock, the addition was about
  16.5 ms. A 156-line GTK-only reproduction also shows the delay on GTK's development branch (4.25.0,
  commit c36202f6, 2026-10-25). Three workarounds using public GTK API were measured; none restored native
  latency, so none ships. The one measured at the real 165 Hz period (`render`) still added about 5.8 ms;
  the other two were measured only with the 60 Hz fallback clock. Settled idle was measured only with
  that fallback clock and stayed quiet. The period was real, but the screen was the headless session's;
  a physical display has still not been measured.
- **On GTK 4.24, scrolling with a held `j` at a 165 Hz headless output drops frames.** Native Neovide drew
  165.5 changed frames a second, Eitri on GTK 4.24.1 140.6, and the same build on GTK 4.22.5 165.4.
  The 99th-percentile frame intervals were 6.1, 12.1 and 6.1 ms respectively. This separate scrolling run
  used stock presentation feedback, so GTK 4.24 still used its 60 Hz fallback clock; scrolling has not been
  repeated with real-period feedback or on a physical display.
- **While a reply streams**, a key's redraw took 11.2 ms at the median with WebKitGTK 2.54 (a stand-in reply),
  against about 2.3 ms with WebKitGTK 2.52.
- **Memory:** one tab used 253 to 283 MiB more than native Neovide, against a goal of 256 MiB, and each extra
  chat view about 28 MB.
- **Idle:** no frames drawn, about 13 ms of CPU a minute. **Startup:** 260 ms, against native's 253 ms.
- **Under load at high refresh rates** the Intel laptop drew 86-93 % of native Neovide's frames at 165 Hz while
  other work kept it 17-28 % busy. That was measured before 0.2.0's typing-latency fix and not since.

## Install

    curl --proto '=https' --proto-redir '=https' --tlsv1.2 -sSfL https://github.com/HunterGrey-cyber/eitri/releases/download/v@VERSION@/install.sh | sh -s -- --version @VERSION@

or install `eitri_@VERSION@_amd64.deb` / `eitri-@VERSION@-1.x86_64.rpm` and run `eitri setup`
once, or install from `eitri-@VERSION@-x86_64-linux.tar.gz` with
`sh install.sh --tarball eitri-@VERSION@-x86_64-linux.tar.gz --sums SHA256SUMS --sig SHA256SUMS.sig`
(or unpack it and run its own `bin/eitri setup` from inside the extracted directory). A `.deb`/`.rpm` upgrade
from 0.2.2 needs that `eitri setup` again: it builds the new agent backend.

Eitri needs `nvim` >= 0.10 somewhere it can find one -- either on `PATH`, or its own private copy
under `XDG_DATA_HOME/eitri/nvim` (never placed on `PATH`; none of the packages depend on a system
nvim). Ubuntu 24.04's own package is 0.9.5, below that floor. `install.sh` (the curl command above)
offers to fetch the official nvim release for you at install time if none new enough is found; run
`eitri setup --nvim-only` on its own at any later time -- after a `.deb`/`.rpm`/tarball install,
or again -- to get the same offer.

## The agent backend is built on your machine

No asset here contains Anthropic's Claude Agent SDK. `eitri setup` downloads it from npm and
builds the agent backend (the sidecar) on your own machine, from `@VERDANDI_ASSET@` (the public
Verdandi at @VERDANDI_REV@) and the official Node release pinned in `RELEASE`. The SDK is not open
source; its own licence applies to your copy of it, and `eitri setup` shows where to read it.

## Licences and source

This program statically links nvim-rs @NVIM_RS_VERSION@, which is licensed under the GNU LGPL-3.0;
its use is covered by that licence. The Corresponding Source for this release is
`@SOURCE_ASSET@` on this page -- **not** GitHub's automatic "Source code" archives, which lack the
Neovide fork submodule, the vendored Rust dependencies and the Skia archive the build linked. It
rebuilds offline, and `SOURCE` (installed at `share/licenses/eitri/SOURCE`) gives the exact recipe
for relinking against a modified nvim-rs. `THIRD-PARTY-LICENSES`, beside it, carries every other
notice.

